  "text": "**Solution 1 is wrong.** For the empty code, $J_\\emptyset$ is the whole ring, since every polynomial vanishes on every codeword in $\\emptyset$. Its canonical form is $\\{1\\}$, not $\\{x_i, 1-x_i\\}$, so the polarized ideal is the unit ideal, not the maximal ideal $\\mathfrak{m}$. The argument therefore doesn't apply, and $2n-1$ is not justified by this code.\n\n**Solution 2 gives the plausible answer $n$, but its reasoning has gaps.**\n- The single-codeword computation is correct: $\\mathcal{P}(J_{\\{v\\}})$ is generated by $n$ distinct variables, a regular sequence. Its quotient $S/\\mathcal{P}$ has projective dimension $n$, so $\\operatorname{pd}(S/\\mathcal{P})=n$. Under the ideal convention, $\\operatorname{pd}(\\mathcal{P})=n-1$ instead, so the solution mixes conventions.\n- The upper bound $\\operatorname{pd}\\le n$ is asserted via \"Hochster formula and properties of the associated simplicial complex\" without a derivation. The supporting examples only check a few small cases.\n\nI recall that the known result in the polarized neural ideal literature is a bound of $n$ attained by some code, which matches Solution 2's answer under the quotient convention. I haven't verified that bound from first principles, so my confidence in $n$ is moderate.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
